WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.670
Steven Cohen had it all.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:06.830
He lived in a 35,000-square-foot mansion in
Greenwich, Connecticut

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.830
He flew to work by helicopter.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:14.830
And he amassed one the largest private art
collections in the world - valued at $1 billion.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:19.000
Including this $8 million shark suspended
in formaldehyde.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.690
Cohen has been jokingly referred to as the
ultimate predator because of his keen trading

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.690
instincts.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:31.349
His hedge fund, SAC Capital, managed $15 billion
dollars and averaged returns of 30% a year

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:32.960
for 20 years!

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.340
It was almost as if Cohen had a crystal ball
and could predict the future.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.840
Except perhaps his own.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.039
His world appeared to come crashing down when
the FBI and SEC accused SAC Capital of insider

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:51.980
trading: Getting confidential, insider information,
trading on it, and making a profit.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.809
This is certainly the most lucrative insider
trading scheme ever charged.

00:00:56.809 --> 00:01:00.100
After a seven-year investigation that began
with the arrest of the founder of another

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:05.910
prominent hedge fund, the feds targeted executives,
traders, analysts, lawyers, and scientists

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:10.460
connected to Wall Street, ultimately hoping
to bring down Cohen.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:16.360
SAC Capital ended up pleading guilty to insider
trading and Cohen’s henchmen went to prison.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.830
Yet Cohen managed to get away scot-free.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.159
With not a single criminal charge.

00:01:21.159 --> 00:01:22.830
How did he do it?

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:27.549
This is the story of how the Feds tried and
failed to bring down the greatest trader who

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:29.410
ever lived.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:34.220
Steven Cohen was born into a middle-class
family on Long Island, the brightest of eight

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.220
siblings.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.860
He mastered poker in high school which taught
him the art of making money.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.880
He wasn’t as well off as his rich classmates
at Wharton business school and felt a need

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:43.939
to prove himself.

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:47.380
It was clear very early on he had a knack
for trading.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.190
“I never saw talent like that.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:53.329
It was just staring at you,” recalled a
friend of Cohen’s who ran options at trading

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:56.590
firm Gruntal, Cohen’s first job out of college.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:01.380
He had the instincts and acted quickly and
made $5 to $10 million a year in commissions

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.399
while still in his early 20s.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:07.130
But…he did get a scare that would affect
him for the rest of his career.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.489
While at Gruntal, he bought shares of RCA
just days before General Electric took over

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:13.950
the broadcaster.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.140
When the SEC investigated him for insider
trading, he pled the fifth and was never charged.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.330
Privately, his ex-wife Patricia said he panicked
and feared ending up in prison.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.010
This close call taught him to be more careful…

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.010
It wasn’t long before he went out on his
own.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.230
In 1992, he launched SAC Capital.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.760
At the time, few knew what a hedge fund was.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.840
In the simplest of terms, it was a way for
rich people to diversify and get stable returns.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:45.480
A fund manager would identify the best companies
and buy their shares while selling short the

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.290
ones that were likely to perform poorly.

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.650
Shorting involves borrowing a stock, selling
it, and hoping to repurchase it at a cheaper

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.680
rate to repay the loan.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.930
This dual strategy of buying some stocks ("long")
and betting against others ("short") provides

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.260
a "hedge" against market fluctuations, hence
the term "hedge fund".

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.730
SAC focused on quick trades, reacting to market
events rather than investing for the long

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.730
term - the opposite of Warren Buffett’s
strategy.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.880
Hedge funds make money off commissions.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.810
Usually a performance fee of 20% of profits
each year.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:22.890
But SAC was so good at what they did that
Cohen charged a whopping 50%!

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.790
The tech boom of the '90s benefited many hedge
funds like SAC as they capitalized on the

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.489
rapid growth in the technology sector and
the stock market's bullish sentiment.

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:36.870
Faced with increased competition, how do you
get an advantage over other hedge funds?

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:41.950
You hire the best and brightest traders and
analysts who could get the inside scoop.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:46.610
Technically, it’s illegal for them to get
proprietary, nonpublic information and use

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.000
it to make trades.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.390
Yet…because of the desire to get an edge
over rivals, this was inevitable, as Sheelah

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.640
Kolhatkar writes in “Black Edge”, the
quest to bring down the most wanted man on

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:58.640
Wall Street:

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.900
“...black edge is like doping in elite-level
cycling or steroids in professional baseball.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.670
Once the top cyclists and home-run hitters
started doing it, you either went along with

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:09.840
them or you lost.”

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:10.939
Everyone was doing it.

00:04:10.939 --> 00:04:13.569
But Cohen was the best at it.

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:16.250
One of his favorite sectors was health care.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.989
He hired a biotechnology specialist from a
small hedge fund in Boston named Mathew Martoma,

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:23.910
who had an MBA from Stanford.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.910
Martoma’s base salary of $200,000 was nothing
compared to the millions he’d make in bonuses

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.620
every year if his trades went well.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.940
Martoma paid close attention to two medical
companies: Elan Corporation and Wyeth.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.979
They were testing a new drug to treat Alzheimer’s,
called bapineuzumab or “bapi” for short.

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:47.420
It was a medication that some speculated would
be the biggest-selling drug of all time.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.729
Martoma was really confident in the drug because
of the information he was getting about it

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:53.220
from a source.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:58.820
SAC Capital used the services of a matchmaking
firm to connect Martoma with a medical expert.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.690
And not just any medical expert.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:06.190
Martoma connected with Dr. Sidney Gilman,
the Chair of the Safety Monitoring Committee

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.070
involved in the clinical trials for bapi.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:14.600
None of this came cheap; experts were compensated
$1,000 for a 30-minute phone call.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.430
Dr. Gilman signed up not for the money but
because he could talk about the work that

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:19.539
he loved.

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:23.560
He made it clear to Martoma that he couldn’t
disclose confidential information:

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:28.400
“Although I know more than is publicly available,
I have a confidentiality agreement and will

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.050
share only information that is openly available,
of course.”

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:38.340
However, as conversations flow and bonds form,
Gilman began sharing.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:42.190
He and Martoma spoke often on the phone, sometimes
for hours.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:46.530
Gilman saw Martoma as the son he lost to suicide.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:51.940
Martoma capitalized on his friendship with
Gilman to nudge him into confidential areas.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:56.560
Martoma wanted to know if the drug had any
side effects that could derail its approval.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.419
Gilman admitted there were side effects of
brain swelling but said it could actually

00:06:00.419 --> 00:06:01.820
be a sign the drug was working.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.190
(Brain swelling detected in regular brain
scans)

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:07.250
Researchers believed Alzheimer’s was caused
by a buildup in the brain of a “sticky protein”

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.919
or plaque called beta-amyloid.

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:11.759
Bapi was designed to attack the plaque.

00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:16.171
And the brain swelling might have been a reaction
to the drug actively working to clear out

00:06:16.171 --> 00:06:17.280
the plaque.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.080
Martoma was so certain that the new drug would
cure Alzheimer’s that he sent Cohen a memo

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.180
with a “Conviction” rating of 9 on a scale
from 1 being not certain and 10 being very

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.520
certain that the drug showed promise.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.060
The rating system was how Cohen’s traders
communicated the value of whatever information

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.460
they were getting without exposing Cohen to
any details of how they knew something.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:44.690
In other words, the rating system protected
Cohen from the behavior of his employees.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.030
Martoma convinced Cohen to build up enormous
positions in Elan and Wyeth securities.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:53.650
Totaling $700 million in value by the spring
of 2008.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:58.100
Interestingly, another group of healthcare
traders at SAC felt the bapi trial was going

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:03.100
to fail and didn’t understand why Cohen
had taken such huge positions in both companies.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.750
Elan, in particular, was the smaller of the
two companies and the riskier bet.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:12.180
And what amplified the gamble was that all
this was happening amidst the catastrophic

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:17.810
financial crisis of 2008, a period marked
by widespread economic collapse.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:22.750
When the skeptical SAC analysts went to Cohen
with concerns about the bapi drug, he waved

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:25.889
them off, accusing them of “pissing on the
parade”.

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:31.030
In July 2008, Dr. Gilman was set to present
the full results of the drug trial at the

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:35.240
annual International Conference on Alzheimer’s
Disease in Chicago.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:38.090
Martoma wanted to know about the results beforehand.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.000
He called up Gilman and said he was going
to be passing by Ann Arbor, Michigan where

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.099
Gilman lived, as his late uncle had passed
away a couple of months before and was buried

00:07:46.099 --> 00:07:47.099
nearby.

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.870
He asked if he could meet Gilman.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:53.560
Gilman agreed and they met at his University
of Michigan office…where he showed Martoma

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:57.930
the PowerPoint presentation he planned to
give at the Alzheimer’s conference.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:02.750
The presentation was marked: “Confidential,
Do Not Distribute.”

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.699
Martoma could tell from the slides that the
results of the drug trial were going to be

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:08.009
disappointing.

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:12.870
Bapi had only been effective for a small group
of patients - those without the gene predisposing

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:14.360
them to Alzheimer’s.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.540
And treatment didn’t get better with more
doses.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:22.070
Although Dr. Gilman felt the drug might have
potential, Wall Street didn’t see it the

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.070
same way.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:26.720
As Martoma explained to Gilman, “The market
doesn’t like a drug that only helps half

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.409
the population.”

00:08:28.409 --> 00:08:33.060
Martoma knew shares of Elan and Wyeth would
crash once news got out.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:38.630
The following day, on July 20, 2008, he typed
an email to Cohen with this subject line:

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.100
Is there a good time to catch up with you
this morning?

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.100
It’s important.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:44.580
Then they spoke on the phone for 20 minutes.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.649
Over the course of the next nine days, SAC
Capital liquidated its entire equity position

00:08:49.649 --> 00:08:55.589
in Elan and almost all of its position in
Wyeth – totaling 17.7 million shares worth

00:08:55.589 --> 00:08:58.200
approximately $700 million.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.060
SAC didn’t want to draw attention to the
selloff and kept trading in low visibility

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:08.450
accounts and “dark pools” - private stock
exchanges where trades were executed anonymously.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:15.080
And on top of that - SAC also shorted nearly
8 million shares of Elan and Wyeth.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:21.899
Through all this, SAC earned profits and avoided
losses of $275 million.

00:09:21.899 --> 00:09:27.430
While this was going on, SAC Capital actually
hired a former SEC chairman to give a talk

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:32.870
about insider trading, which is beyond ironic!

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:37.930
When news came out that the drug was a failure,
shares of Elan and Wyeth plummeted.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:43.560
In three days, Elan dropped from $33.75 to
below $10.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:48.740
Other traders at SAC thought it would spell
disaster for the company only to learn that

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:52.019
SAC had sold its positions in Elan and Wyeth.

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:56.579
When Dr. Gilman presented the findings at
the Alzheimer’s conference, he was recovering

00:09:56.579 --> 00:09:57.990
from cancer.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.560
Given everything that had happened, he couldn’t
believe that Martoma hadn’t checked in on

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:03.560
him.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.560
He sent him an email:

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:05.560
“Hi, Mat.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.110
I haven’t heard from you in a while and
hope that all is well with you...I just wonder

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:10.110
how you’re faring.”

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:17.370
Meanwhile, Martoma received a $9 million bonus
for his excellent work on the drug trades.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:21.899
But if SAC didn’t think anyone would notice
the trades - they were wrong.

00:10:21.899 --> 00:10:27.310
A trader at RBC Capital Markets noticed the
unusual activity and made a complaint to a

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:29.080
regulatory body.

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:34.380
By this time, regulators were already starting
to unravel the intricate web of insider trading

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.391
within the hedge fund industry.

00:10:35.391 --> 00:10:42.011
In 2009, they arrested Raj Rajaratnam, the
founder of the hedge fund Galleon Group, on

00:10:42.011 --> 00:10:43.820
securities fraud charges.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.640
And this was just the tip of the iceberg.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.790
They targeted a network that they hoped would
eventually strike the bull’s eye: Steven

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:53.089
Cohen.

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:57.690
When the Wall Street Journal published an
article in November 2010 titled: “U.S. in

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:03.520
Vast Insider Trading Probe”, it blew the
quiet investigation out of the water.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:08.640
After former SAC portfolio manager Donald
Longueuil read the article, he panicked and

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:13.040
destroyed his USB and hard drives containing
incriminating evidence and threw them into

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.639
random garbage trucks in the middle of the
night.

00:11:15.639 --> 00:11:20.000
He confessed this to his best friend, fellow
SAC manager Noah Freeman.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.440
What Longueuil didn’t realize was that Freeman
had become an FBI informant to save himself.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:28.180
He was wearing a wire and recorded their conversation.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.140
Longueuil ended up pleading guilty to insider
trading charges.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.230
However, his conviction didn’t bolster the
FBI’s case against Cohen as there was not

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.980
much to implicate him.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.279
The FBI kept digging.

00:11:41.279 --> 00:11:45.650
Another suspicious case involved a SAC analyst
hearing that Dell’s earnings were going

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:47.279
to be a disappointment.

00:11:47.279 --> 00:11:53.279
SAC research analyst Jon Hovarth was tipped
off by an analyst friend, who got it from

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:58.329
an analyst at another hedge fundwho obtained
the information from an insider at Dell.

00:11:58.329 --> 00:12:03.300
Horvath relayed the tip to his boss at SAC,
portfolio manager Michael Steinberg.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.420
Horvath wrote:

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:08.250
“I have a 2nd hand read from someone at
the company…”

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:12.029
Steinberg responded with discretion: “please
be discreet.”

00:12:12.029 --> 00:12:16.660
Steinberg then shorted his shares of Dell,
pocketing a profit of $1 million.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:22.570
And two days before Dell reported earnings,
Cohen sold his entire long position of 500,000

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:23.620
shares.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:27.880
After earnings came out, Cohen complimented
Steinberg: “Nice job on dell.”

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:29.870
Steinberg responded: “Thanks.

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:31.180
This ole dog can still hunt.”

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:36.730
Horvath’s email not only reached Steinberg
but also another manager, who passed it on

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:41.410
to another portfolio manager, who then shared
it with…Steven Cohen.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:45.540
The Feds thought this was the proof they needed
to connect Cohen to insider trading.

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:48.570
However, Cohen’s defense believed otherwise.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:53.000
Cohen’s lawyer, Marty Klotz, argued that
Cohen received thousands of emails in his

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.860
inbox daily, making it unlikely he even saw
this particular one.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:01.930
Even if he had, there's no clear evidence
it amounted to insider trading since Cohen

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:04.130
didn’t know where the tip came from.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:09.810
On December 18, 2013, a jury found Steinberg
guilty of insider trading.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:14.019
He was so overwhelmed he fainted before the
verdict was delivered.

00:13:14.019 --> 00:13:17.310
He was sentenced to three and a half years
in prison.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.790
Steinberg had refused to cooperate with authorities
and as a result, they didn’t have enough

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:23.360
to nail Cohen.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.600
This was frustrating for FBI agents who understood
that Cohen knew all and controlled all.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:34.779
As former SAC analyst C.B. Lee described to
the FBI, the operational structure at SAC

00:13:34.779 --> 00:13:39.570
resembled a bicycle wheel: the spokes represented
the portfolio managers with their teams of

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:44.410
analysts and traders, and at the hub, was
Steven Cohen.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:48.880
Since Steinberg had refused to cooperate,
the Feds only hope was the pharmaceutical

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:51.010
case involving Martoma.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:56.070
Phone records had shown Martoma was the SAC
employee in touch with Dr. Gilman.

00:13:56.070 --> 00:14:00.940
The FBI was certain Martoma would flip on
Cohen to avoid a lengthy prison sentence of

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:02.570
up to ten years.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:06.940
Yet, surprisingly, Martoma refused to turn
against Cohen.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:12.070
Even after SAC fired him for being a “one-trick
pony”, unable to replicate his past success

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:13.750
with Elan and Wyeth.

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:18.750
He pleaded the Fifth, refusing to incriminate
himself or his boss.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:24.269
It’s worth noting that Martoma’s lawyer
was on SAC’s payroll.

00:14:24.269 --> 00:14:26.756
Authorities then questioned Cohen.

00:14:27.205 --> 00:14:32.494
While he admitted to investing in Elan based
on Martoma’s advice, he never specified

00:14:32.494 --> 00:14:34.514
what that advice was.

00:14:34.514 --> 00:14:37.164
Cohen never mentioned Dr. Gilman.

00:14:37.164 --> 00:14:43.284
The prosecution painted Dr. Gilman as the
victim - a lonely older man who had been charmed

00:14:43.284 --> 00:14:44.753
by a young trader.

00:14:44.753 --> 00:14:49.214
As a result, he saw his career crumble as
he resigned from the University of Michigan

00:14:49.214 --> 00:14:50.813
in disgrace.

00:14:50.813 --> 00:14:55.344
When asked what set Martoma apart from the
other investors he dealt with, Gilman replied:

00:14:55.344 --> 00:14:56.623
“He was personable.

00:14:56.623 --> 00:15:00.194
And he, unfortunately, reminded me of my first
son.

00:15:00.194 --> 00:15:03.344
In his inquisitiveness.

00:15:03.344 --> 00:15:04.344
His brightness.

00:15:04.344 --> 00:15:06.204
And, sadly, my first son was very bright also.

00:15:06.204 --> 00:15:09.183
And committed suicide.”

00:15:09.183 --> 00:15:14.094
After a bit of hesitation, he mentioned that
when FBI agent Kang first approached him,

00:15:14.094 --> 00:15:19.224
“The agent also mentioned that I am only
a grain of sand, as is Mr. Martoma.

00:15:19.224 --> 00:15:22.914
They are really after a man named Steven A.
Cohen.”

00:15:22.914 --> 00:15:29.134
And yet, Martoma refused to testify against
his former boss in exchange for a lighter

00:15:29.134 --> 00:15:30.183
sentence.

00:15:30.183 --> 00:15:34.024
One can only speculate why, but there are
three lines of thought:

00:15:34.024 --> 00:15:39.363
He didn’t want to snitch, which is honorable
yet it was seemingly at odds with his questionable

00:15:39.363 --> 00:15:41.264
actions earlier in life.

00:15:41.264 --> 00:15:46.354
It came out in court that he had been expelled
from Harvard Law School for altering his transcripts

00:15:46.354 --> 00:15:48.954
when applying for federal clerkships.

00:15:48.954 --> 00:15:53.794
The second possibility was fear of retribution
from Cohen, though there was no evidence Cohen

00:15:53.794 --> 00:15:55.964
had any ties to mobsters.

00:15:55.964 --> 00:15:59.464
The third possible reason for staying mum
had to do with money.

00:15:59.464 --> 00:16:04.574
The case had ruined Martoma financially - he
had to give his house in Boca Raton, Florida,

00:16:04.574 --> 00:16:06.714
and millions of dollars of his own money.

00:16:06.714 --> 00:16:09.534
Did he hope that Cohen would repay his loyalty?

00:16:09.534 --> 00:16:14.884
Mathew Martoma’s father Bobby had another
explanation, saying said his son believed

00:16:14.884 --> 00:16:19.533
in the Ninth Commandment: “Thou shalt not
bear false witness against thy neighbor.”

00:16:19.533 --> 00:16:23.563
The jury has come back with a verdict and
it is guilty.

00:16:23.563 --> 00:16:27.224
It is a verdict against 39-year-old Mathew
Martoma.

00:16:27.224 --> 00:16:32.934
For the verdict to come back this quick in
two full days is unusually quick.

00:16:32.934 --> 00:16:36.454
Mathew Martoma was found guilty of securities
fraud.

00:16:36.454 --> 00:16:39.454
He was sentenced to nine years in prison.

00:16:39.454 --> 00:16:44.964
On the day Martoma was supposed to start his
sentence, Cohen spent $101 million at Sotheby’s

00:16:44.964 --> 00:16:49.254
to buy an Alberto Giacometti sculpture called
the Chariot.

00:16:49.254 --> 00:16:52.884
Steven Cohen was never charged with insider
trading.

00:16:52.884 --> 00:16:57.994
The SEC dropped that charge against him and
instead charged him with “failure to supervise”

00:16:57.994 --> 00:16:59.984
Steinberg and Martoma.

00:16:59.984 --> 00:17:04.414
The SEC worried about how it would look to
lose to the head of one of the world’s most

00:17:04.414 --> 00:17:08.664
powerful hedge funds in light of a lack of
concrete evidence.

00:17:08.664 --> 00:17:13.494
Cohen also never faced any criminal charges,
though his firm, SAC Capital, pled guilty

00:17:13.494 --> 00:17:18.734
to insider trading charges and paid $1.8 billion
in fines.

00:17:18.734 --> 00:17:23.564
As part of the settlement, SAC agreed to stop
managing outside money and convert into a

00:17:23.564 --> 00:17:28.523
family office, which meant Cohen could only
manage his own personal wealth of $10 billion

00:17:28.523 --> 00:17:31.924
- so he was far from being out of the game.

00:17:31.924 --> 00:17:37.164
SAC Capital rebranded to Point72 Asset Management.

00:17:37.164 --> 00:17:41.294
While his underlings went to prison, life
went on for Steven Cohen.

00:17:41.294 --> 00:17:45.414
He bought the New York Mets in 2020 for $2.4
billion.

00:17:45.414 --> 00:17:46.864
He continued to buy art.

00:17:46.864 --> 00:17:49.004
And kept on trading.

00:17:49.004 --> 00:17:54.574
The family office was allowed to reopen its
doors to outside investors in 2018.

00:17:54.574 --> 00:17:59.724
Last year, it generated $2.4 billion in profit.

00:17:59.724 --> 00:18:05.714
When I was a child, I remember my mom worked
overtime nearly every night to make ends meet.

00:18:05.714 --> 00:18:11.124
Despite my parents' limited means, they still
saved up for my weekly piano lessons, a testament

00:18:11.124 --> 00:18:12.234
to their love for me.

00:18:12.234 --> 00:18:17.184
But I often wished they had someone to help
them better manage their finances in a new

00:18:17.184 --> 00:18:18.184
country.

